### TL;DR

An elongated radiant cut diamond is a rectangular brilliant-cut diamond with a length-to-width (L/W) ratio above 1.20. It gives you the fire and sparkle of a round brilliant inside a long, finger-flattering outline — with no bow-tie risk and a lower clarity budget than an emerald cut.

**The one number that matters:** Target an L/W ratio of **1.25–1.35**. Below 1.20, it won't look elongated. Above 1.40, it starts looking narrow.

**The core insight:** Two named concepts define this shape. **The Hybrid Effect** — the elongated radiant is the only rectangular diamond that combines an emerald cut's long face-up silhouette with a round brilliant's 70-facet fire. **The Elongation Threshold** — the 1.20–1.40 L/W ratio window that makes it actually elongated.

→ Start here: [1ct natural G-VS2 elongated radiant at $2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) + [Classic Four Prong Platinum setting at $1,180](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/classic-four-prong-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-platinum-item-194156?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) = **$3,498 total ring**. Lab-grown: [1ct E-VVS1 at $867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) + setting = **$2,047 total ring**.

**Priority:** Check the L/W ratio on the GIA certificate before anything else. Retailers use "elongated" loosely. A ratio of 1.12 is not elongated. The certificate number is the only truth.

---

## What Is an Elongated Radiant Cut Diamond?

The elongated radiant cut is a rectangular brilliant-cut diamond with a length-to-width ratio above 1.20. It looks like an emerald cut at first glance — long, rectangular, flat-edged, with clipped corners. But the mechanics underneath are completely different.

An emerald cut uses step facets: wide, parallel planes that reflect light in sheets. That creates the hall-of-mirrors effect — elegant and glassy. An elongated radiant uses 70 brilliant-cut facets across both the crown and pavilion. That creates fire and scintillation — light fracturing in all directions, not pooling.

I'm Farzana Hasan, GIA Expert at [Diamond Critics](/about-farzana/). The elongated radiant is one of the most technically undervalued shapes in the current market. This guide covers what the ratio means, where prices sit by carat, and exactly what to buy.

---

## The Hybrid Effect

![Elongated radiant cut diamond face-up silhouette showing the rectangular outline and 70 brilliant-cut facets](/images/elongated-radiant-cut-lw-ratio-guide.avif)

The elongated radiant cut carries two design advantages that no other rectangular diamond shape delivers at the same time.

**Advantage one: the long face-up silhouette.** The rectangular outline spreads more surface area across the finger than a round or square diamond of the same carat weight. A 1ct elongated radiant at 1.30 L/W measures approximately 6.8mm × 5.2mm — versus 6.4mm across for a 1ct round brilliant.

The elongated radiant covers more finger, looks larger, and elongates the hand. That is exactly the effect oval buyers are chasing.

**Advantage two: 70-facet fire.** Unlike the emerald cut's step facets, the elongated radiant applies full brilliant-cut faceting to the entire stone — crown and pavilion both. The result is aggressive light return. Sparkle, not reflection. Fire, not glass.

No other rectangular shape does both. Oval cuts are brilliant-cut but curved, not rectangular. Emerald cuts are rectangular but step-cut, not brilliant. The elongated radiant is the only shape that is simultaneously rectangular and fully brilliant-cut. That is The Hybrid Effect.

**Why it matters for buyers:** You get the finger-elongating silhouette of an oval. You get the geometric outline of an emerald. And you get 70-facet brilliance that neither of those shapes can match. At equivalent specs, the elongated radiant frequently prices below both.

---

## The Elongation Threshold

The Elongation Threshold is the L/W ratio range of 1.20 to 1.40 that defines an elongated radiant cut. Below this window, the stone is square or near-square. Above it, the stone starts looking narrow rather than elongated.

Here is how the full ratio spectrum breaks down:

| L/W Ratio | Shape | What You See on the Finger |
|---|---|---|
| 0.95–1.05 | Square radiant | Compact, nearly equal sides — princess cut energy |
| 1.06–1.19 | Tweener radiant | Slightly rectangular but not clearly elongated |
| **1.20–1.35** | **Elongated radiant (sweet spot)** | **Visibly elongated — the shape most buyers want** |
| 1.36–1.45 | Extended elongated | Narrow and long — flattering on wide hands |
| 1.46+ | Too elongated | Can look fragile or thin |

**The sweet spot is 1.25–1.35.** At 1.25, the elongation is clearly visible. At 1.33, it's maximally flattering on most finger sizes. Above 1.40, the stone starts to look pinched in four-prong settings.

**Where to find the ratio:** Your GIA certificate lists measurements as "L × W × D" in millimeters near the top. Divide length by width. That is your L/W ratio. That number is the only thing that matters — not what the retailer calls the stone.

**What sellers don't tell you:** "Elongated radiant" is a marketing term, not a graded category. A stone listed as elongated might have a ratio of 1.12. Always check the certificate.

