This is the table most buyers are looking for before they decide. All clarity/color specs at the VS2 / G-H tier for natural stones, F-VVS1 for lab — a true apples-to-apples comparison.

| Factor | Elongated Radiant | Oval Cut | Emerald Cut | Princess Cut |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Facets** | 70 | 56–58 | 57 | 50–58 |
| **Cut style** | Brilliant | Brilliant | Step-cut | Brilliant |
| **Bow-tie risk** | None | Yes — every oval | None | None |
| **Clarity floor** | VS2 | VS2 | VS1 | VS2 |
| **Color floor (natural)** | H | H | G | H |
| **Finger elongation** | ✅ Strong | ✅ Strong | ✅ Moderate | ❌ None |
| **Corner chip risk** | Low (clipped) | None (curved) | Low (clipped) | High (sharp) |
| **Inclusion hiding** | Excellent | Good | Poor | Good |
| **Price vs round (same specs)** | −15 to −25% | −10 to −20% | −10 to −20% | −20 to −30% |
| **GIA cut grade issued?** | No | No | No | No |
| **Best setting** | Rectangular 4-prong | Round/oval prong | Rectangular 4-prong | Square prong or bezel |

**The verdict from this table:** The elongated radiant beats oval on facet count and bow-tie risk. It beats emerald cut on inclusion forgiveness and clarity budget. It beats princess cut on corner safety. The only shape it doesn't clearly beat is oval for buyers who want soft, curved edges — that's a style preference, not a quality difference.

