## Elongated Radiant vs Oval: The Real Difference

Both shapes elongate the finger. Both are brilliant-cut. Both look bigger per carat than round diamonds. They are not the same shape.

**The bow-tie problem.** Every oval cut has a bow-tie shadow — a dark area across the center of the stone where light escapes rather than reflects. Some bow-ties are subtle; some are severe. You never know which you have until you look at the stone in motion.

The elongated radiant has zero bow-tie risk. The rectangular outline and facet geometry prevent it entirely.

**Facet count.** Oval cuts use 56–58 facets. Elongated radiant cuts use 70. More facets, more surfaces for light to break across, more scintillation. In a side-by-side comparison, the radiant throws more sparkle per second as it moves.

**Silhouette.** Oval edges are curved — soft, romantic, feminine. Elongated radiant edges are straight, with clipped corners — geometric, modern, architectural. This is a style preference, not a quality judgment. If you want soft, buy an oval. If you want geometric with maximum fire, the elongated radiant wins.

**Price.** Oval cuts are trending hard right now. That demand pushes oval prices up. An elongated radiant at equivalent specs often runs 10–20% below a comparable oval. That gap buys a better stone or a better setting.

See the full comparison: **[Radiant Cut vs Emerald Cut Diamond](/radiant-cut-vs-emerald-cut-diamond)**

