## Elongated Radiant vs Emerald Cut

![Elongated radiant cut vs oval vs emerald cut — face-up silhouette and light behavior comparison](/images/elongated-radiant-vs-square-radiant-comparison.avif)

They share a silhouette. Everything else is different.

**Fire vs. mirror.** Emerald cuts use step-cut facets — fewer, larger planes that reflect light in clean, parallel sheets. It is beautiful. Reserved. The hall-of-mirrors effect. Elongated radiant cuts use 70 brilliant-cut facets that scatter light in every direction. You get sparkle and fire, not reflection and depth. Neither is better — they serve completely different tastes.

**Clarity cost.** Emerald cuts require VS1 clarity or better in most cases. The step facets act like windows — you look through the stone, not into scattered light, and inclusions are visible. Elongated radiant cuts work well at VS2 because the brilliant facets scatter light across 70 surfaces, masking small inclusions. That clarity gap has a real price impact — VS1 stones cost 20–30% more than VS2 at equivalent color and carat.

**Bottom line.** Buy an emerald cut if you want the hall-of-mirrors look and are willing to pay for VS1 clarity. Buy an elongated radiant if you want fire, are comfortable at VS2, and want the same rectangular silhouette at a lower clarity premium.

---

## Clarity Rules for Elongated Radiant Cut

**The floor is VS2.** The 70-facet brilliant-cut pattern is the most effective inclusion-masking architecture in diamond cutting. A VS2 elongated radiant will look eye-clean to the naked eye in normal lighting.

**SI1 is possible, not guaranteed.** Some SI1 elongated radiant cuts are eye-clean. But this must be verified stone-by-stone through a video inspection or in person. Never buy an SI1 sight-unseen from a photo.

**VVS and above is wasted money.** There is no visible optical benefit to buying VVS in an elongated radiant. The brilliant facets make VVS, VS1, and VS2 look identical face-up. That budget is better spent on a larger stone or a better color grade.

**Where inclusions matter most.** Center-table inclusions are the most dangerous — they sit in the most visible zone of the stone. Edge inclusions covered by prongs are hidden by the setting. A VS2 with an edge inclusion is a better buy than a VS1 with a center-table feather. Always check the inclusion map on the certificate before buying.

---

## Color Guide for Elongated Radiant Cut

**Natural diamonds in white gold or platinum:** H is the floor. G-H is the sweet spot — eye-white without paying the D-F premium. Below H, body color can collect at the corners and long edges of the rectangular stone. Above F, you are paying for a difference that's invisible in normal light.

**Natural diamonds in yellow or rose gold:** Go as low as J. The warm metal reflects warmth into the stone — an I or J color looks perfectly natural in yellow gold. This is where the elongated radiant hides real value. A J-VS2 in yellow gold saves 20-25% over a G-VS2 and shows no visible difference.

**Lab-grown in any metal:** Buy F or better. Lab-grown prices at D-F are compressed close to each other. There's no reason to buy an H-color lab radiant when D-VVS1 is available at a small premium — the gap has effectively closed in the lab market.

