## The 3/4 Carat Problem

This is specific to elongated radiant cuts and most buyers don't know it exists.

When you search for a 0.75ct elongated radiant with an L/W ratio above 1.25, inventory is thin. The geometry is the reason. At 0.75ct, producing a 1.28 L/W radiant means a stone roughly 5.8mm × 4.5mm — a difficult cut with limited market demand, so fewer are produced and certified.

What you find at 0.75ct are mostly near-square shapes (L/W 1.05–1.15) that retailers still label "radiant." They look nothing like the elongated shape you're picturing.

The fix is simple. Go up to 0.90ct — inventory opens immediately. Or buy lab-grown: a [1ct lab E-VVS1 at $867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) gives you more face-up size and more elongation than a 0.75ct natural, at a lower total price. That is the better buy every time.

